"Organizes people's thinking and intentions into a research-based skyline and compares that model with available organizational support.\n\nUse when: Find gaps between people's goals and existing offerings. Plan a service around how people think rather than internal features.\n\nAvoid when: The team only needs an interface sitemap or a quick assumed journey.\n\nVisual grammar: Grouped concepts form towers and broader mental spaces above a baseline. People's thinking, reactions and guiding principles define the upper structure. Support offerings below the baseline reveal alignment and gaps.\n\nBuild: Gather detailed accounts of people's reasoning in the selected problem space. Synthesize intent-based concepts into coherent towers and mental spaces. Map existing support below the baseline and investigate unsupported needs.\n\nCheck: Upper concepts describe people's thinking rather than product features. Grouping follows research evidence and meaningful intent. The support inventory is separate from the research-derived model.\n\nPitfalls: Calling any mind map a mental model diagram. Organizing towers around the provider's departments instead of human intent.\n\nExample: A household budgeting study groups planning, reassurance and tradeoff thinking, then compares those towers with existing financial education support.\n\nAlternatives: Affinity diagram, Experience map."
